+++ b/qualification/native-frontier-design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# Offline native extension feasibility, not an installed authority
+
+Pinned Incus a99413afd0c742da3bba10eb4f75d639f9dbbd32.
+
+## Proposed narrow transaction boundary
+
+A native cluster-database extension can persist a request intent before body processing and atomically record database-row authorization in the SAME transaction that creates its native instance row. Seal must atomically close epoch acceptance AND invalidate unconsumed birth grants. An accepted-before-seal handler is retained as unresolved but cannot create a new instance row after seal. Already committed row grants remain charged and unknown, not physically terminal. This is stronger than the external HTTP proxy's acceptance frontier, but does not stop OS side effects from paths that bypass this gate.
+
+Prototype API accepts an existing *sql.Tx, never opens a separate ledger: Accept(epoch, request ID); Seal(epoch); ClaimRow(epoch, request ID, generated incarnation, native row ID) after row INSERT but before transaction commit. Failed row claim rolls back the whole transaction, including row INSERT. A separate native table binds a never-reused generated incarnation to native instance ID; volatile.uuid is NOT authority (CreateInternal accepts it from args.Config and restore can copy it). Request state remains retained. No release method.
+
+Deletion design: acquire the existing native per-instance operation lock; reread native instance ID AND new immutable incarnation under the database transaction, then persist a deletion intent before external storage/device cleanup. Reject mismatch before touching storage. Hold identity/name reservations through cleanup; all rename/restore/start/recreate paths must reject the reserved identity. Final DB removal alone is insufficient: lxc.delete invokes pool.DeleteInstance, device cleanup and backups before deleting the DB row, so an SQL conditional DELETE after cleanup is too late. Uncertain cleanup retains deleting/charged and forbids name reuse. No automatic release on DB absence.
+
+## Concrete pinned insertion points
+
+- cmd/incusd/instances_post.go instancesPost: durable acceptance before body Decode (authenticated route context still required).
+- internal/server/instance/instance_utils.go CreateInternal: same DB transaction around cluster.CreateInstance; snapshot branch requires separate coverage and is excluded initially.
+- internal/server/instance/drivers/driver_lxc.go Delete: identity recheck AFTER operation lock, BEFORE d.delete side effects. The internal d.delete path bypasses that lock and therefore must also require a typed delete grant; otherwise no qualification.
+- internal/server/db/cluster/instances.mapper.go: generated SQL currently deletes/renames by name; new incarnation table cannot alone protect pre-DB physical operations.
+
+## Explicit incomplete coverage
+
+CREATE from empty/image/copy/migration/backup, snapshots, restore, rename/move, start/restart/autostart, device hotplug, cluster forwarding, database recovery and internal calls all need traced gates. VMs/QEMU, clustered multi-node mode, snapshots/restores, arbitrary admin/SQL writes are excluded from the initial candidate and must be DENIED, not silently ignored. Local socket/CLI/admin execution cannot be controlled by this test extension alone. Fresh installation enrollment must reject prior unknown requests/resources; snapshot rollback requires external anti-rollback generation. A native epoch stored in the same database still rolls back with it.
+
+## Prototype boundary
+
+First executable slice runs pure Go/SQLite tests in this pinned source tree against transaction-bound row records, seal races, SQL rollback/unknown ACK, and identity mismatch refusal. It will not be wired into the daemon or claim covered native side effects. Full daemon compilation/runtime is a separate gate (Incus requires native libraries and privileges). A passing transaction prototype does not qualify an installation, permit deletion, or release capacity.
+
+## Post-commit native side effects are not row birth
+
+Pinned createAsEmpty commits CreateInternal BEFORE pool.CreateInstance/backup. Native reverts can remove a database row with storage cleanup incomplete. The experiment therefore calls its result `row_bound`, not physical birth, and retains incarnation/request history WITHOUT cascading native-row foreign keys. No row absence or completed SQL transaction is a terminal physical outcome. A native extension must also gate post-commit storage/driver work, retain unresolved outcomes across errors/reverts, and fence replay/cleanup. ClaimRow alone cannot close the native frontier.
+
+The synthetic native row contains a current native_token separate from permanent incarnation history; the guard requires BOTH to match. Deleted rows and reused numeric IDs reject old delete intents. This models a needed new immutable native identity column, not existing Incus schema or config. Only same-transaction identity intent is tested; no physical deletion is performed.
+
+Pinned Incus's instances schema uses AUTOINCREMENT. The explicit numeric-ID replacement fixture represents an out-of-band/restore mutation and is NOT evidence of normal committed Incus row-ID reuse. The absent-row guard matters independently. This experiment deliberately does not apply schema migrations or wire guards into native handlers.
