+# Pinned native integration coverage: all live paths currently unqualified
+
+Source: Incus 6.0.4 a99413afd0c742da3bba10eb4f75d639f9dbbd32. This is a conservative implementation map, NOT a completed call-graph or authorization proof. No daemon hook is installed. Initial restricted profile must deny every unsupported mutation, including internal callbacks, rather than merely hiding API routes.
+
+| Mutation family | Concrete source boundary | Current executable coverage | Still required |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Empty creation | `cmd/incusd/instance.go:instanceCreateAsEmpty`; `instance_utils.go:CreateInternal`; `backend.go:CreateInstance` | Exact hashed upstream orchestration body transplanted with two dependency substitutions; actual upstream revert helper, fake row/storage dependencies | Native accepted intent propagation, same cowsql row transaction, durable pre-storage attempt, actual driver outcome |
+| Image creation | `instance.go`; `backend.go:CreateInstanceFromImage` | Source inspection only | Image download/cache effects, driver create and revert obligations |
+| Copy/migration | `instances_post.go`, `instance.go`, `backend.go:CreateInstanceFromCopy/FromMigration` | None | Per-resource accepted work across source/target, both sides of migration and snapshots |
+| Backup import | `backend.go:CreateInstanceFromBackup` | Source comment confirms storage BEFORE native instance-row creation | Gate before unpack/storage; row-only gate insufficient |
+| Restore/snapshots | `driver_lxc.go:Restore/Snapshot`; `backend.go:RestoreInstanceSnapshot/CreateInstanceSnapshot` | None | Identity generation changes, snapshot and storage mutation frontier |
+| Rename/move | `instance_post.go`; `driver_lxc.go:Rename`; `backend.go:RenameInstance` | None | Stable incarnation reservation before any name-targeted action |
+| Start/restart/autostart | `instance_state.go`; `instances_put.go`; `driver_lxc.go:Start` and stop callback | Source read confirms stop callback invokes Start(false) | Deny or gate reboot/autorestart before process creation |
+| Delete/ephemeral cleanup | `instance_delete.go`; `driver_lxc.go:Delete/delete`, stop callback; `backend.go:DeleteInstance` | SQL identity-intent fixture only; empty-create cleanup failure counterexample | Native operation lock and immutable identity recheck BEFORE physical cleanup; internal delete bypass must share authority |
+| Rebuild | `driver_common.go:rebuildCommon` | Source read shows DeleteInstance then CreateInstance/FromImage | One retained operation spanning destruction and recreation |
+| Internal recovery/import | `api_internal.go`; `api_internal_recover.go` call CreateInternal | None | Deny restoration/import in restricted domain or include native accepted work and external rollback anchor |
+| VM/QEMU | `driver_qemu.go` | None | Excluded, must deny; container cgroup coverage does not establish VM scope |
+| Device/storage/cluster/admin | native update/hotplug, storage drivers, cluster forwarding, local SQL/root | None | Whole-asset membership, all writers, driver coverage and independent enforcement |
+
+GitHub ephemeral/JIT runner registration is NOT Incus `instance.ephemeral`. Native ephemeral guests may call internal `d.delete(true)` after stop without the public DELETE handler. A GitHub completion signal or exhausted JIT token cannot stand in for native deletion history.
+
+## Executed upstream orchestration boundary
+
+`empty_path_test.go` transplants the exact pinned `instanceCreateAsEmpty` function body. Only its signature and two dependency constructor calls are adapted. A hash and normalized source-body comparison prevent silently replacing its control flow. It uses upstream `shared/revert` but row, pool, operation and instance objects are fixtures, not Incus daemon/storage execution.
+
+Tests reproduce (1) storage created then error/unknown ACK, leaving physical state while row cleanup runs; (2) backup failure whose deferred forced-delete cleanup fails and is ignored, also leaving physical state with no row; (3) upstream `defer instOp.Done(err)` captures earlier nil, so lock completion does not prove native terminal outcome. A simulated durable-intent failure blocks storage before attempt. None of these is authoritative physical release evidence.
+
+## Proposed phase contract, not implemented in daemon
+
+1. Native request accepted durably before dispatch/body-dependent work, retaining unparsed intents too.
+2. Native row grant and immutable incarnation bind in same database transaction; seal denies any unconsumed row grant.
+3. Before EACH potentially effectful storage/process/device call, persist a unique suboperation intent and consume a fencing generation. Native epochs/ownership must also be enforced against direct/internal paths.
+4. Record response independently from completion; errors, crash, missing ACK and failed reverts retain unknowns. Cleanup is a separately journaled mutation, not an unobserved `revert.Hook`.
+5. Only a qualified driver/resource adapter may issue durable terminal physical outcomes. Never infer them from row removal, operation-lock completion, timeout, swallowed cleanup error or a successful outer HTTP reply.
+6. Even all known local suboperations terminal does not prove sole ownership or anti-rollback history. Unsupported paths and out-of-band writers must be prevented by installation/native enforcement before any authority is exposed.
+
+Feasibility: the transaction and orchestration ordering can be tested offline. Full pinned daemon compilation/runtime remains unperformed; this environment currently lacks lxc, libdqlite and libacl pkg-config entries. These tests do not justify enabling an allocator, destructive finalizer or automatic capacity reuse.
+
+## Compile-only probe result
+
+A subsequent `go test -c -o /tmp/incusd-frontier-build-probe.test ./cmd/incusd` was attempted against the pinned source (no daemon/test binary was launched). It failed with missing `lxc.pc`, `cowsql.h`, `libudev.h`, and `sys/capability.h`. The pinned source uses cowsql; the earlier generic libdqlite pkg-config probe is not the actual compilation diagnosis. A disposable native build image providing those development dependencies is needed before daemon-level hook tests. No packages or system services were installed or changed by this probe.
+
+## Next insertion map: three pinned source contracts, not installed hooks
+
+`mutation_boundaries_test.go` parses SHA-256-pinned upstream Go ASTs. It executes no native driver. These checks preserve the inspected negative facts when updating the prototype; passing them does not qualify daemon mutation coverage or authorize capacity release.
+
+| Pinned method | Observed boundary | Necessary insertion and retained obligation |
+|---|---|---|
+| `backend.CreateInstanceFromBackup` | `driver.CreateVolumeFromBackup` runs before the `postHook` closure containing `VolumeDBCreate` is even defined. The backend comment separately documents storage before instance-row creation. | Persist accepted import/suboperation identity **before** native unpack/storage. Do not wait for the row hook. A returned error, crash, missing driver ACK, or failed rollback leaves the import charged/unknown, including volumes/snapshots without instance rows. The AST test does not prove all callers or inspect actual storage. |
+| `common.rebuildCommon` | `DeleteInstance`, then either empty/image creation, then config DB update, all for an existing instance. There is no `instance.CreateInternal` call in this method. | Bind one durable rebuild identity to the existing incarnation before deletion; record distinct destructive and birth attempts without releasing the parent reservation between them. Every failure/unknown result remains charged. Updating config or completing a row transaction is not native physical completion. Full caller/lock/restore analysis is still required. |
+| `lxc.onStop` | A goroutine can invoke `Start(false)` on reboot/autorestart and `delete(true)` for an Incus-ephemeral guest, without a new public API request. | Gate these native transitions or reject this guest/profile capability under an enforceable native installation. A public HTTP proxy alone cannot be sole ingress. Their retained operation identities must survive API request exit and daemon crash. Merely setting GitHub one-job registration does not disable native reboot or ephemeral cleanup. |
+
+The assertions use actual syntax-node nesting and source positions. They do not execute the ordering, demonstrate error-path completion, instrument cowsql, or establish ownership/anti-rollback guarantees. The existing exact empty-create transplant remains the only orchestration slice executed with the upstream revert helper; its physical state is still simulated. All paths in the coverage table remain **unqualified**.
+
+## Offline storage-attempt seam (rebuild prefix only)
+
+`storage_attempt_test.go` executes the exact pinned `rebuildCommon` storage-call prefix against fixture pool methods. The prefix has only fixture signature/`err` declaration/final-return scaffolding; a pinned-source/body comparison checks the delete/empty-or-image-create control flow. No config transaction, native pool, cowsql or LXC operation lock is executed.
+
+The proposed insertion records a SQLite attempt before the fixture call, keyed by accepted request, bound existing incarnation and ordered `delete`/`create` phase. Duplicate phases cannot auto-reissue after an error or crash. Creation cannot start without an observed successful delete return. That ordering condition is only control flow, **not** a physical deletion certificate. Parent and attempt history remain after every return. Outcomes are deliberately named `returned_success`/`returned_error`, never `physically_absent` or `released`.
+
+Tests cover side effect followed by lost ACK, failure recording the returned outcome across database reopen, an execution gap after durable attempt but before native call, interruption between delete/create, wrong target/out-of-order phase, and a callback that performs its effect after sealing. The last counterexample is intentional: this journal cannot revoke an already admitted callback. It also cannot prevent a different writer changing the incarnation after its SQL check; a real shared native lock/fencing mechanism remains required. Simulated crash points discard fixture execution, not kill a real daemon.
+
+Restore and internal lifecycle transitions need analogous accepted-operation identities **before** their native calls, but are not implemented by this rebuild-only seam. Backup import has no existing instance row to bind at its first storage call and therefore needs a separate resource-scope identity contract; this helper rejects unsupported phases rather than treating that case as qualified. Database history retention is not a physical capacity reservation, native fence, safe rollback, automatic recovery/resume protocol or release authority. There is no release API.
+
+## Offline backup error-prefix negative
+
+`backup_prefix_test.go` source-compares the actual prefix beginning at `importRevert := revert.New()` through the symlink-error return. It executes the upstream `shared/revert` helper with fake storage, then deliberately returns a fixture sentinel before the remaining import and posthook logic. It is not a successful backup-import implementation.
+
+The prefix returns immediately on `CreateVolumeFromBackup` error, before registering any reverter returned alongside that error. A fixture storage effect followed by an unknown/error response therefore survives with no registered cleanup. After a successful driver return, a later symlink failure invokes the registered `revert.Hook`; the hook has no error return, and a simulated failed cleanup can leave storage present while the caller receives only the symlink error. These are counterexamples to treating an outer error or executed reverter as proof of absence, not a claim that any particular stock storage driver necessarily exhibits those injected failures.
+
+No instance/volume database row is constructed in this prefix fixture. The rebuild experiment's existing-incarnation binding cannot qualify this import path. Required next work is an accepted import identity bound to the native storage domain before unpack, complete subresource membership (including snapshots), and independently enforced writer/epoch/physical completion semantics. The before-call failure case here is injected only; it does not implement that durable import contract. Unknown native effects must remain unresolved in any future capacity integration.
+
+## Executed internal stop callback tail (offline only)
+
+`nativefrontier/lifecycle_tail_test.go` compares and executes the exact pinned
+`lxc.onStop` tail beginning at `// Reboot the container`. The function signature
+and enclosing `err` declaration are fixture scaffolding; lifecycle notification
+and scheduler calls are replaced with counters. Start and internal delete are
+fake callbacks. The real earlier stop cleanup, operation-lock creation,
+`shouldAutoRestart` calculation and goroutine are NOT executed. Tests supply the
+already-computed `target`, `autoRestart` and native `ephemeral` fields.
+
+The exact branch is retained: reboot OR autoRestart calls Start and returns,
+even when the native instance is ephemeral; otherwise native ephemeral guests
+call internal `delete(true)`, not the public Delete entrypoint. A deterministic
+fixture queue illustrates that an API-return/ingress-seal marker does not fence
+that internal callback. These markers are not a native fence implementation.
+Injected restart/delete effects followed by errors remain ambiguous. Neither
+fake success nor error proves actual driver completion, absence, operation
+terminality, or that additional callbacks cannot follow. GitHub ephemeral/JIT
+registration is unrelated to this Incus instance flag.
+
+Next real insertion must cover the earlier callbacks and operation locks,
+revalidate native identity before effects under the same lock as all other
+writers, and durably retain every accepted attempt and unknown outcome. The
+public API-only frontier still cannot qualify these native internal paths.
+No live hook, capacity accounting/release API or production code was changed.
